{title2=Mathematical Models of Financial Derivatives}

= 4
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Apply <Itô's formula> to $\log X$ for positive initial value $x_0$. The drift correction is $-\sigma^2/2$, so
$$
\boxed{X_t=x_0\exp\{(\mu-\tfrac12\sigma^2)t+\sigma W_t\}.}
$$
This is <geometric Brownian motion>. Under the physical probability measure, $W_T/\sqrt T$ has the <standard normal distribution>. For positive volatility,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_T<m)=\Phi\left(\frac{\log(m/x_0)-(\mu-\sigma^2/2)T}{\sigma\sqrt T}\right).}
$$
The drift here is $\mu$, because this is a physical bankruptcy probability. The <risk-neutral measure> will instead give drift $r$ for pricing. If $\sigma=0$, the value is deterministic and the default probability is the indicator of $x_0e^{\mu T}<m$; the usual positive-volatility convention is used in the remaining formulas.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

In the <Merton model>, the total equity payoff is $(X_T-m)^+$, exactly a <European call option> on the company value with strike $m$. Total debt pays $\min(X_T,m)=X_T-(X_T-m)^+$. The first payoff has slope zero below $m$ and slope one above it; the second has slope one below $m$ and is constant above it.

\Image[2009-capital-structure-payoffs.png]
{title=Total debt and equity payoffs in units of the debt face value}

Let
$$
d_1=\frac{\log(x_0/m)+(r+\sigma^2/2)T}{\sigma\sqrt T},\qquad d_2=d_1-\sigma\sqrt T.
$$
The <Black-Scholes formula> gives total equity value $C=x_0\Phi(d_1)-me^{-rT}\Phi(d_2)$. Because the total company value is a traded asset, replication gives total debt value $x_0-C$. Divide by the numbers of securities to obtain
$$
\boxed{\text{one share}=\frac{x_0\Phi(d_1)-me^{-rT}\Phi(d_2)}n,}
$$
$$
\boxed{\text{one bond}=\frac{x_0}m\Phi(-d_1)+e^{-rT}\Phi(d_2).}
$$
These are prices per security; the payoff diagram represents the two aggregate claims.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

As insurance against <credit default>, the payment is the positive shortfall of recovery below face value:
$$
H=\left(1-\frac{X_T}m\right)^+=\frac1m(m-X_T)^+.
$$
Thus the <credit default swap> in this question is one $m$th of a <European put option> on the firm value. Its price is
$$
\boxed{V_{\rm CDS}=e^{-rT}\Phi(-d_2)-\frac{x_0}m\Phi(-d_1).}
$$
Equivalently, adding this insurance to one corporate bond produces a sure unit payment, so its price is $e^{-rT}$ minus the bond price from part (b). This also fixes the positive orientation of the printed “difference”: insurance compensates face value minus recovery.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

At maturity, one stock share is worth $(X_T-m)^+/n$. For a nonnegative strike $K$, the <digital call option> therefore pays one exactly on
$$
\frac{(X_T-m)^+}n>K\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad X_T>m+nK.
$$
Under the <risk-neutral measure>, $\log(X_T/x_0)$ is normal with mean $(r-\sigma^2/2)T$ and variance $\sigma^2T$. The <risk-neutral pricing> consequently gives
$$
\boxed{V_0=e^{-rT}\Phi\left(\frac{\log(x_0/(m+nK))+(r-\sigma^2/2)T}{\sigma\sqrt T}\right).}
$$
The effective company-value strike is $m+nK$, because both the outstanding debt and the number of shares matter. At $K=0$, the strict positive-share event remains $X_T>m$.

= 5
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Write $\tau=T-t$. In the <Black-Scholes formula>, both $d_1$ and $d_2$ depend on $x,K$ only through $\log(x/K)$. Replacing them by $\lambda x,\lambda K$ leaves that ratio unchanged. Each prefactor in the put formula acquires the same factor $\lambda$, giving
$$
p(t,\lambda x;\lambda K,T)
=\lambda Ke^{-r\tau}\Phi(-d_2)-\lambda x\Phi(-d_1)
=\boxed{\lambda p(t,x;K,T).}
$$
This is degree-one <homogeneity> of the <European put option> price in its spot and strike together.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Differentiate the homogeneity identity from part (a) with respect to $\lambda$ at $\lambda=1$. The <chain rule> gives <Euler homogeneous function theorem> in the form
$$
xp_x+Kp_K=p.
$$
Using the supplied <put option> spot derivative $p_x=-\Phi(-d_1)$ and the given price,
$$
Kp_K=p-xp_x
=Ke^{-r(T-t)}\Phi(-d_2).
$$
For positive strike, division by $K$ gives the <put-price strike derivative>
$$
\boxed{p_K=e^{-r(T-t)}\Phi(-d_2).}
$$
It is nonnegative, as increasing the strike increases the terminal put payoff.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

At $T_1$, with underlying spot $s$, the <call option on a put option> pays
$$
H(s)=\bigl[p(T_1,s;K_2,T_2)-K_1\bigr]^+.
$$
Let $D=K_2e^{-r(T_2-T_1)}$. The underlying <put option> value is strictly decreasing and convex in $s>0$, with limits $D$ at zero and zero at infinity. If $0<K_1<D$, there is a unique $s_*$ at which that put value equals $K_1$. Hence the compound option payoff is a decreasing convex curve from $D-K_1$ to zero on $[0,s_*]$, followed by a horizontal zero segment. There is an upward kink at $s_*$. If $K_1\geq D$, the payoff is identically zero.

\Image[2009-call-on-put.png]
{title=A call on a put has positive payoff only below an exercise threshold}

For $K_1=0$, the holder obtains the underlying put at no cost, so the payoff is its full value at $T_1$. The discounted put price is a <martingale> under the <risk-neutral measure>. Using the <tower property> in the <risk-neutral pricing>,
$$
\boxed{V(t,s)=p(t,s;K_2,T_2),\qquad t<T_1.}
$$
Explicitly, this is $K_2e^{-r(T_2-t)}\Phi(-d_2)-s\Phi(-d_1)$ with $d_1,d_2$ evaluated over $T_2-t$. The intermediate choice time disappears when its exercise cost is zero.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Write $p(s)=p(T,s;K_3,T_3)$ and $c(s)=c(T,s;K_3,T_3)$. Because the right can be left unexercised, the <chooser with positive exercise cost> has payoff
$$
\boxed{H(s)=\max\{p(s)-K,\ c(s)-K,\ 0\}.}
$$
Let $\tau=T_3-T$ and $D=K_3e^{-r\tau}$. <Put-call parity> gives $c(s)-p(s)=s-D$. Thus the put is selected for $s<D$ and the call for $s>D$; the two are equal at $s=D$. Before truncation at zero, the payoff is decreasing on the left and increasing on the right.

At the meeting point, the <Black-Scholes formula> gives
$$
p(D)=c(D)=M=D\{2\Phi(\sigma\sqrt\tau/2)-1\}.
$$
If $K<M$, the payoff has a positive minimum $M-K$ at $D$. If $K=M$, it touches zero there. If $M<K<D$, each side has a distinct exercise threshold and the payoff is zero between them. If $K\geq D$, even the left-end put value cannot cover the exercise cost, and only the right call branch can be positive. The illustration shows the middle case, including the competing underlying option values.

\Image[2009-chooser.png]
{title=Chooser payoff with an exercise cost above its central option value}

= 6
{parent=2009/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

If $d_1>d_2$, buy $1/100$ of stock 1 and sell $1/100$ of stock 2. Initial cost is zero; the one-period payoff is $u_1-u_2>0$ in the up state and $d_1-d_2>0$ in the down state. This is an <arbitrage>. If terminal rather than time-one arbitrage is required, reinvest the positive receipts in either positive-price stock.

For $d_1<d_2$, define the one-period <Arrow state prices>
$$
\pi_u=\frac{d_2-d_1}{u_1d_2-u_2d_1},\qquad
\pi_d=\frac{u_1-u_2}{u_1d_2-u_2d_1}.
$$
The denominator is positive because $u_1/u_2>1>d_1/d_2$. Both state prices are strictly positive and satisfy $\pi_u u_i+\pi_d d_i=1$ for $i=1,2$. Thus every portfolio's present value equals the positive state-price-weighted value of its two future payoffs.

Use the same strictly positive pricing rule at every node of the two-period tree. A nonnegative terminal payoff that is positive in at least one state has strictly positive price by backward induction, so a zero-cost <self-financing strategy> cannot generate an arbitrage. Therefore **the market is arbitrage free when $d_1<d_2$**, despite having no initially supplied bond. This is a <two-stock binomial market without a supplied bond>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For holdings $h_1,h_2$, a sure payment of 100 requires
$$
140h_1+120h_2=100,\qquad80h_1+90h_2=100.
$$
Subtracting gives $2h_1+h_2=0$, so $h_1=-1$ and $h_2=2$. Thus sell one share of stock 1 and buy two shares of stock 2. Its initial cost is $100(-1+2)=100$.

This <synthetic zero-coupon bond> pays exactly its initial cost after one period, and therefore
$$
\boxed{B(0)=100,\qquad r=\log(100/B(0))=0.}
$$
At later nodes the holdings can be rescaled to generate the same one-period risk-free return; a physical bond need not be listed separately.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

With the rate $r=0$, the <risk-neutral probability in a binomial market> must satisfy
$$
q u_i+(1-q)d_i=1,\qquad i=1,2.
$$
The first stock gives $0.8+0.6q=1$, hence $q=1/3$. The second gives $0.9+0.3q=1$, confirming the same answer. Thus
$$
\boxed{(q,1-q)=(1/3,2/3).}
$$
Take the successive up/down choices independent under this measure, so both stock prices are <martingales>. The martingale transition condition applies from times zero to one and one to two; the displayed $t+1$ condition in the source should be read for these two transitions.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

The <exchange option> has terminal payoffs determined by the two successive up/down moves:
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{path}&S_1(2)&S_2(2)&(S_1(2)-S_2(2))^+\\ \hline
uu&196&144&52\\
ud,\ du&112&108&4\\
dd&64&81&0
\end{array}
$$
The two stocks span both one-step states, so this finite market is complete. The <risk-neutral pricing> uses the independent transition probabilities $q=1/3$ and $1-q=2/3$. Since the synthetic risk-free rate is zero,
$$
V_0=\frac19\,52+2\left(\frac13\frac23\right)4+\frac49\,0
=\boxed{\frac{68}{9}}.
$$
The price is approximately 7.56 in the currency units of the stock prices.
